in @prism
in @meta
in @io
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/property
in @epistemologic/property/content_addressed

# @epistemologic/silicon/arch/arm64
#
# The ARM64 architecture as a typed carrier. Names what an observer
# CAN SEE when they look at the substrate's instruction set. Same
# observer-relative discipline as the rest of @epistemologic: the
# type is the manifest; the detection primitive populates it from
# the running system; the literal property verifies the detection
# matches reality.
#
# Per docs/specs/shard-design.md §4: this grammar is one of the four
# type carriers the @mirror/shard.shard record composes from. The
# silicon field of a shard holds an arm64 (or x86_64) value; Fate's
# |\> tournament reads that field as part of its input set.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-pipe-hole-and-au-binary.md: the
# instruction subset Fate picks for a |\> binary depends on which
# extensions are present in the running arm64 silicon (NEON, SVE/SVE2,
# AMX on Apple Silicon, BF16/FP16, crypto). This carrier names them
# so the tournament has typed handles instead of stringly tags.
#
# The detection body is a hole filled by Fate at boot via tournament
# against @code/kernel/arm64; the carrier ships with the contract,
# not the implementation, matching the tick-1 phase in the design
# doc's §10 implementation order.
#
# Substrate-pull placement (2026-06-06, Mara's silicon migration):
# moved from boot/std/epistemologic/silicon/arch/ to shards/. grammar
# block becomes prism block per
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]].
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/silicon/arch/arm64.mirror` declares
# `@epistemologic/silicon/arch/arm64` and only that.

prism @epistemologic/silicon/arch/arm64 {
  focus arm64
  project arm64
  split arm64
  shift arm64
  settle arm64
}

# ISA generation. ARMv8 baselines and ARMv9 generation. The values
# are the only ones the substrate distinguishes; finer-grained
# point releases (v8.4 vs v8.5) collapse into the nearest tagged
# generation because Fate's tournament only branches at the
# boundaries the substrate actually cares about.
type isa_version = armv8_0 | armv8_2 | armv8_5 | armv9_0

# ISA extensions. Each extension is a typed flag the running
# silicon either has or does not. NEON is the SIMD baseline; SVE/
# SVE2 are the variable-length successors; AMX_v1/v2 are Apple's
# matrix coprocessor generations (v1 = M1, v2 = M2/M3); BF16/FP16
# are precision-format extensions; crypto bundles AES + SHA1/2.
type extension =
    neon
  | sve
  | sve2
  | amx_v1
  | amx_v2
  | bf16
  | fp16
  | crypto

# Vendor. Who builds the silicon. The set is closed by the
# substrate's current deployment surface: Apple's M-series, AWS
# Graviton, Ampere Altra, ARM-designed reference cores. Adding a
# new vendor means adding the variant; no stringly fallback.
type vendor = apple | arm | aws_graviton | ampere_altra

# Microarchitecture. The concrete core identifier. Closed sum, one
# variant per shipped microarch the substrate has touched. The
# `generic` variant is the conservative fallback for unrecognized
# cores; Fate's tournament treats it as the worst-case subset.
type micro =
    apple_m1
  | apple_m2
  | apple_m3
  | graviton2
  | graviton3
  | altra
  | generic

# The arm64 carrier. Five fields name what the observer sees when
# they look at the ARM64 silicon: which ISA generation, which
# extensions are present, who built it, which microarch, and the
# pointer width (always 64 on arm64 — the field is here so the
# carrier is uniformly shaped with x86_64 and any future arch).
# Endianness is fixed little for arm64 in the deployment surface
# this substrate targets; not a field because there is no choice
# to surface.
type arm64 = {
  isa:        isa_version,
  extensions: [extension],
  vendor:     vendor,
  micro:      micro,
  pointer:    u32,
}

requires content_addressed(arm64)

# Detection primitive. Reads the running system for ARM64 features.
# On Darwin: @io.exec("sysctl", ["-n", "hw.optional.arm64", ...])
# surfaces NEON, AMX, etc. On Linux: @io.exec("cat",
# ["/proc/cpuinfo"]) surfaces the Features line. Both ride the
# same @io.exec primitive that already exists; no new Rust shim
# required. The body is a Fate-resolved hole: the tournament
# picks the right detection chain against the runtime OS, fills
# the carrier's fields, and returns the typed value.
detect() -> arm64 { \ }

# Literal property. Does the declared identity hold under
# measurement? Per @epistemologic.literal: the carrier IS what
# it says iff the detection primitive's output matches a re-read
# of the running silicon. The verifier runs detect() twice and
# checks the two values content-address to the same OID. Hash
# composition is non-expansive (per @epistemologic/property/
# content_addressed); same silicon, same OID, same verdict.
property literal(s: arm64) -> verdict { \ }

out @epistemologic/silicon/arch/arm64
out arm64
out isa_version
out extension
out vendor
out micro
out detect
out literal
